WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.782
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:07.989
Hello, my name is Llya Strebulaer,

00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:09.343
and I'm a professor at

00:00:09.343 --> 00:00:10.698
the Graduate School of

00:00:10.698 --> 00:00:12.695
Business Stanford University,

00:00:12.695 --> 00:00:14.905
specializing in venture capital and

00:00:14.905 --> 00:00:15.991
private equity.

00:00:15.991 --> 00:00:17.846
One of the classes I co-teach is

00:00:17.846 --> 00:00:19.979
Angel and Venture Capital Financing

00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:22.053
for Entrepreneurs and Investors.

00:00:22.053 --> 00:00:24.751
Today are five takeaways from this

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:25.407
class,

00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:30.818
[MUSIC]

00:00:30.818 --> 00:00:33.319
Success in startup investing is not

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:35.650
just due to luck as many believe,

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.899
but also skills,

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:38.663
sweet, perseverance.

00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:40.661
If you see me spend over a hundred

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:42.216
hours on due diligence or

00:00:42.216 --> 00:00:44.215
just one making dozen reference

00:00:44.215 --> 00:00:45.917
calls to check on the idea,

00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:48.212
founders, product, market, they

00:00:48.212 --> 00:00:50.434
debate startups opportunities and

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:53.190
risks with their partners.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.003
The skills of how to conduct due

00:00:55.003 --> 00:00:56.678
diligence are critical as is

00:00:56.678 --> 00:00:58.700
the appreciation that it is hot and

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.250
often unglamorous work.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.782
[MUSIC]

00:01:05.782 --> 00:01:07.997
If investors conduct such extensive

00:01:07.997 --> 00:01:10.212
due diligence, founders need to get

00:01:10.212 --> 00:01:11.645
fully prepared for it.

00:01:11.645 --> 00:01:13.931
As I summarize to my students to be

00:01:13.931 --> 00:01:16.137
founders, ask yourself all the

00:01:16.137 --> 00:01:18.588
questions if you're an investor,

00:01:18.588 --> 00:01:20.630
then your aim is to have a great

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.938
answers to all these questions.

00:01:22.938 --> 00:01:25.194
Depth is absolutely critical.

00:01:25.194 --> 00:01:28.650
Also, identify your weak spots.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:29.953
Investors avoid shallow

00:01:29.953 --> 00:01:31.813
founders who don't know the ins and

00:01:31.813 --> 00:01:33.960
outs of their startup.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.560
As my former student who now is

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.416
successful investor told me, if I

00:01:37.416 --> 00:01:39.272
come away from meeting the founder

00:01:39.272 --> 00:01:41.064
without learning anything new,

00:01:41.064 --> 00:01:42.035
it's a bad sign.

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:44.146
[MUSIC] Founders should remember,

00:01:44.146 --> 00:01:46.308
success is all about execution.

00:01:46.308 --> 00:01:50.189
Most investors would rather invest

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:54.068
in A+ teams, pursuing B+ ideas,

00:01:54.068 --> 00:01:57.819
rather than B+ teams pursuing A+

00:01:57.819 --> 00:01:58.738
ideas.

00:01:58.738 --> 00:02:01.042
[MUSIC]

00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:02.886
The third takeaway is that you

00:02:02.886 --> 00:02:04.286
need to know the ins and

00:02:04.286 --> 00:02:06.437
outs of contracts in the VC space.

00:02:06.437 --> 00:02:08.351
These contracts determine how any

00:02:08.351 --> 00:02:10.133
proceeds from startups outcome

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:11.130
are determined.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.174
That is, who is paid what, when,

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:14.934
how, and how the decisions in

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.250
startups life are made.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.739
We actually spent most of our

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:20.042
course, discussing all

00:02:20.042 --> 00:02:22.790
these contracts in great detail.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.456
Financial security and startups

00:02:24.456 --> 00:02:26.003
are very unusual if you compare

00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:27.431
them to traditional debt and

00:02:27.431 --> 00:02:29.480
equity securities.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.583
They need to take into account that

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:33.615
many startups fail that startups

00:02:33.615 --> 00:02:35.646
raise many rounds of funding from

00:02:35.646 --> 00:02:36.806
many investors and

00:02:36.806 --> 00:02:39.810
those investors need patience.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.889
The takeaway is to understand this

00:02:41.889 --> 00:02:43.825
contract and contractual terms

00:02:43.825 --> 00:02:45.265
really, really well.

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:51.781
[MUSIC]

00:02:51.781 --> 00:02:53.770
Another takeaway is the utmost

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.142
importance of corporate governance

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.207
that is how decisions are made in

00:02:58.207 --> 00:02:58.981
startups.

00:02:58.981 --> 00:03:01.188
For example, lead early stage VCs

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:03.397
typically become board members,

00:03:03.397 --> 00:03:05.462
get shareholder voting right.

00:03:05.462 --> 00:03:07.210
Often they get special veto rights

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.717
to prevent the startup taking

00:03:08.717 --> 00:03:09.983
certain actions without

00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:10.777
the approval.

00:03:10.777 --> 00:03:12.595
This rights help protect investors

00:03:12.595 --> 00:03:14.475
who are often minority shareholders

00:03:14.475 --> 00:03:16.172
in the startup and are afraid they

00:03:16.172 --> 00:03:17.324
could be expropriated

00:03:17.324 --> 00:03:18.968
by others such as the founders.

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:21.316
Founders may also be concerned that

00:03:21.316 --> 00:03:23.273
VCs may kick them out once they

00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:25.237
control the startups board.

00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:27.263
Understanding corporate galas,

00:03:27.263 --> 00:03:29.638
it's implications, and negotiations

00:03:29.638 --> 00:03:31.382
around it is really a critical

00:03:31.382 --> 00:03:33.284
skill in the world of startups.

00:03:33.284 --> 00:03:40.316
[MUSIC]

00:03:40.316 --> 00:03:42.036
A surprising result is that

00:03:42.036 --> 00:03:43.458
valuation serves many

00:03:43.458 --> 00:03:45.553
purposes apart from just finding

00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:47.048
out what the security or

00:03:47.048 --> 00:03:49.442
company farewell is, especially for

00:03:49.442 --> 00:03:50.962
early stage startups.

00:03:50.962 --> 00:03:52.852
Then, in startups is often

00:03:52.852 --> 00:03:55.315
about how to divide the future pie,

00:03:55.315 --> 00:03:57.452
the world of startups even uses

00:03:57.452 --> 00:03:58.615
its own jargon.

00:03:58.615 --> 00:04:00.102
Instead of valuation used

00:04:00.102 --> 00:04:02.266
elsewhere, the post money valuation

00:04:02.266 --> 00:04:03.889
or the pre-money valuation

00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:06.020
is used for startups.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.495
In class, we'll discuss at length

00:04:07.495 --> 00:04:09.570
these and other valuation terms.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.542
Remember one thing that the post

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:13.587
money valuation is not the same as

00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:15.960
the fair or the market value.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.107
In fact, my research shows that

00:04:18.107 --> 00:04:20.414
post money valuation is typically

00:04:20.414 --> 00:04:22.322
higher than the fair value,

00:04:22.322 --> 00:04:24.070
a result with far reaching

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.360
practical implications.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.009
So if you take all these five

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:29.373
takeaways to heart and

00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:31.454
utilize them either as a founder,

00:04:31.454 --> 00:04:33.678
or as an investor, then you're much

00:04:33.678 --> 00:04:35.759
more likely to succeed in the world

00:04:35.759 --> 00:04:37.986
of angel venture capital investment

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:40.030
in the world of startups.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:40.584
Good luck.

00:04:40.584 --> 00:04:50.584
[MUSIC]

00:04:59.184 --> 00:05:01.977
If anybody would have told me even

00:05:01.977 --> 00:05:04.570
20 years ago that I would ever

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.677
teach wench capital, I would love.

00:05:07.677 --> 00:05:09.574
But now that I teach wench capital

00:05:09.574 --> 00:05:11.606
and private equity, I think that is

00:05:11.606 --> 00:05:13.571
really the most amazing thing to do

00:05:13.571 --> 00:05:14.655
to meet founders,

00:05:14.655 --> 00:05:16.758
investors who are really passionate

00:05:16.758 --> 00:05:18.384
about what they do and trying

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:20.213
to help them both at Stanford and

00:05:20.213 --> 00:05:21.814
outside is truly amazing.

00:05:21.814 --> 00:05:31.814
[MUSIC]

